<div class="editorial-home">
  <main class="editorial-column">
    <h1>Build serious business software without rebuilding the platform underneath it.</h1>

    <section class="editorial-section editorial-section--first" aria-labelledby="why-dygo">
      <h2 id="why-dygo">Why dygo exists</h2>
      <div class="editorial-prose">
        <p>Business software carries data that people depend on. It needs permissions, stable schema changes, background work, operating history, secure configuration, and an interface that stays coherent as the system grows.</p>
        <p>Teams rebuild that foundation for every product. Agentic coding makes the first version faster, but it also makes it easier to invent a new structure in every file and leave the hard operational work until later.</p>
        <p>dygo gives the application a fixed shape. Builders define the business. The framework handles the repeated platform work.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="editorial-section" aria-labelledby="model">
      <h2 id="model">The model</h2>
      <div class="editorial-prose">
        <p><strong>Apps</strong> own business modules. They contain Entities, access policy, Fixtures, Hooks, Jobs, Schedules, and explicit database Patches.</p>
        <p><strong>Entities</strong> describe business Records in readable YAML. Fields, links, collections, indexes, constraints, route identity, and Record naming stay visible in source control.</p>
        <p><strong>Go code</strong> owns behavior that needs a compiled boundary. Record Hooks run inside the transaction. Job handlers run through durable PostgreSQL-backed Job Executions.</p>
        <p><strong>Studio</strong> reads the same metadata and renders the operating surface. People work with Records, forms, lists, and Activity without each App shipping a separate admin interface.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="editorial-section" aria-labelledby="foundation">
      <h2 id="foundation">What the framework handles</h2>
      <div class="editorial-prose">
        <p><strong>Data.</strong> Metadata-driven PostgreSQL schema, stable Record identity, links, collections, indexes, constraints, and explicit Patches for unsafe changes.</p>
        <p><strong>Access.</strong> Session authentication, one-Administrator bootstrap, global roles, and server-side Entity permissions for Record actions.</p>
        <p><strong>Work.</strong> Transactional Record Hooks, durable Jobs, queue Workers, retry policy, timeouts, idempotency keys, and recurring Schedules.</p>
        <p><strong>Operations.</strong> Health checks, encrypted project secrets, Activity history, diagnostic Logs, schema snapshots, and CLI diagnostics.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="editorial-section" aria-labelledby="loop">
      <h2 id="loop">The basic loop</h2>
      <p>Start a project, prepare PostgreSQL, create the first Administrator, and open Studio.</p>
      <ol class="editorial-commands" aria-label="dygo quickstart commands">
        <li><span>›</span><code>dygo new my-system</code></li>
        <li><span>›</span><code>cd my-system</code></li>
        <li><span>›</span><code>dygo db prepare</code></li>
        <li><span>›</span><code>dygo setup</code></li>
        <li><span>›</span><code>dygo dev</code></li>
      </ol>
      <p><a href="/start-here/quickstart/">read the quickstart →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="editorial-section" aria-labelledby="principles">
      <h2 id="principles">The opinions</h2>
      <div class="editorial-prose">
        <p><strong>Speed needs structure.</strong> Generators and coding agents can move quickly inside conventions that keep data, access, and operations predictable.</p>
        <p><strong>Metadata must stay readable.</strong> Important behavior belongs in files that people and agents can inspect without reverse-engineering the runtime.</p>
        <p><strong>Permissions come early.</strong> The server protects business data. Interface checks help the user, but they do not replace enforcement.</p>
        <p><strong>Database danger stays explicit.</strong> Migration is additive. Renames, drops, type changes, and backfills move through reviewed Patches.</p>
        <p><strong>Studio is product UI.</strong> Shared metadata-driven screens still need consistent language and interaction.</p>
      </div>
      <p><a href="/concepts/doctrine/">read the doctrine →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="editorial-section" aria-labelledby="current">
      <h2 id="current">What exists today</h2>
      <p>dygo is in early framework development. APIs and file formats can change before the first stable release.</p>
      <ul class="editorial-list">
        <li><span>›</span> project, App, Entity, Hook, Job, Fixture, and test generators</li>
        <li><span>›</span> PostgreSQL schema sync and app-owned Patches</li>
        <li><span>›</span> generic authenticated Record APIs</li>
        <li><span>›</span> flat role-based Entity permissions</li>
        <li><span>›</span> compiled transactional Record Hooks</li>
        <li><span>›</span> durable Jobs, Workers, and Schedules</li>
        <li><span>›</span> encrypted project secrets</li>
        <li><span>›</span> Studio login, navigation, lists, forms, details, and Activity</li>
      </ul>
      <p><a href="/reference/status/">read the framework status →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="editorial-section" aria-labelledby="next">
      <h2 id="next">Where it is going</h2>
      <p>The metadata foundation will support more business behavior without changing the App model.</p>
      <ul class="editorial-list editorial-list--planned">
        <li><span>○</span> Workflow actions, approvals, and state transitions</li>
        <li><span>○</span> row-level and Field-level access rules</li>
        <li><span>○</span> saved Views, Reports, and custom Studio Pages</li>
        <li><span>○</span> specialized Job and Schedule operating screens</li>
        <li><span>○</span> OAuth, SSO, API keys, invitations, and password recovery</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="editorial-section" aria-labelledby="source">
      <h2 id="source">Source available</h2>
      <div class="editorial-prose">
        <p>dygo is released under the O'Saasy License. You can use it, modify it, self-host it, and build business software with it. Competing hosted services where dygo itself is the primary value are reserved.</p>
        <p>The framework is being built in public. Contributions to framework code are paused while the first stable shape is being established. Documentation and website contributions are welcome.</p>
      </div>
      <p><a href="https://github.com/hapyco/dygo">view the source on GitHub →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="editorial-section editorial-closing" aria-labelledby="start">
      <h2 id="start">Start with one Entity</h2>
      <p>Define one useful business Record. Prepare the database. Open it in Studio. Add behavior only when the business needs it.</p>
      <p><a href="/build-apps/entities/">define an Entity →</a></p>
